<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Paul's Church, Frankfurt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Paulskirche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Paul's Church, the Paulskirche, is a former Lutheran church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main, built between 1789 and 1833. From May 1848 to May 1849 it was the meeting place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a>, the first freely elected all-German national assembly, which drafted a constitution for a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> there. Burnt out in 1944 and rebuilt by 1948, it is used for exhibitions and ceremonies as a national symbol of democracy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-pauls-church-frankfurt.jpg" alt="St. Paul's Church, Frankfurt" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: Andreas Praefcke · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frankfurt_Paulskirche_au%C3%9Fen_2011a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
